              "type": "text",
              "content": "194"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.482,
            0.055,
            0.87,
            0.073
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "VII. Banach Algebras and Spectral Theory"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.126,
            0.1,
            0.871,
            0.213
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ProOF. We have already seen that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\alpha / M"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a Banach space and, as was mentioned prior to the statement of the theorem, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\alpha / M"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an algebra. If "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x , y { \\in } { \\mathcal { A } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "u , v \\in \\mathcal { M }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "(x + u)(y + v) = xy + (xv + uv + uv) \\in xy + \\mathcal{M}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Hence "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\| (x + \\mathcal{M})(y + \\mathcal{M}) \\| = \\| xy + \\mathcal{M} \\| \\leqslant \\| (x + u)(y + v) \\| \\leqslant \\| x + u \\| \\| y + v \\|"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Taking the infimum over all u, v in M gives that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\| ( x + \\mathcal { M } ) ( y + \\mathcal { M } ) \\| \\leq \\| x + \\mathcal { M } \\|"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\| y + \\mathcal { M } \\|"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . The remainder of the proof is left to the reader."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.125,
            0.229,
            0.871,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "It may be that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\alpha / M"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has an identity even if  does not. For example, let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { A } = C _ { 0 } ( \\mathbb { R } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { M } = \\{ \\phi \\in C _ { 0 } ( \\mathbb { R } ) : \\phi ( x ) = 0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " when "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "| x | \\leqslant 1 \\}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . If "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\phi _ { 0 } \\in C _ { 0 } ( \\mathbb { R } )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " such that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\phi _ { 0 } ( x ) = 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "| x | \\leqslant 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\phi _ { 0 } + \\mathcal { M }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an identity for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\sigma / d"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . In fact, if "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\phi \\in C _ { 0 } ( \\mathbb { R } ) , \\quad ( \\phi \\phi _ { 0 } - \\phi ) ( x ) = 0 \\quad \\mathrm { i f } \\quad | x | \\leqslant 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Hence "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( \\phi + \\mathcal { M } ) ( \\phi _ { 0 } + \\mathcal { M } ) = \\phi + \\mathcal { M }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (see Exercises 6 through 9)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.339,
            0.233,
            0.356
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "EXERCISES"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.358,
            0.871,
            0.41
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\varkappa"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be a Banach algebra and let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { L }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be all of the closed left ideals in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\varkappa"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "I _ { 1 } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "I _ { 2 } \\in \\mathcal { L } ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " define "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "I_{1} \\lor I_{2} \\equiv \\mathrm{cl}(I_{1} + I_{2})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "I _ { 1 } \\wedge I _ { 2 } = I _ { 1 } \\cap I _ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Show that with these definitions "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { L }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a complete lattice with a largest and a smallest element."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.417,
            0.871,
            0.486
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Let X be locally compact. For every open subset U of X, let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "I ( U ) = \\{ \\phi \\in C _ { 0 } ( X ) \\}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\phi = 0 \\mathrm { o n } X \\backslash U \\}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Show that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "U   \\mapsto   I ( U )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a lattice monomorphism of the collection of open subsets of X into the lattice of close ideals of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "C _ { 0 } ( X )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . (It is, in fact, surjective, but the proof of that should wait.)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.494,
            0.871,
            0.546
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "( X , \\Omega , \\mu )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be a σ-finite measure space and let I be an ideal in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "L ^ { \\infty } ( X , \\Omega , \\mu )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " that is weak* closed. Show that there is a set ∆ in Ω such that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "I = \\{ \\phi \\in L ^ { \\infty } ( X , \\Omega , \\mu ) :"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\phi   =   0"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " a.e. on "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\Delta \\}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.135,
            0.55,
            0.871,
            0.61
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { A } = \\left\\{ \\left[ \\begin{matrix} { \\alpha } & { 0 } \\\\ { \\beta } & { \\alpha } \\\\ \\end{matrix} \\right] : \\alpha , \\beta \\in \\mathbb { F } \\right\\}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " and let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { M } = \\left\\{ \\left[ \\begin{matrix} { 0 } & { 0 } \\\\ { \\beta } & { 0 } \\\\ \\end{matrix} \\right] : \\beta { \\in } \\mathbb { F } \\right\\}"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Show that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\alpha"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a Banach algebra and M is a maximal ideal in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\varkappa"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.618,
            0.806,
            0.636
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Show that for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "n \\geqslant 1,   M_n(\\mathbb{C})"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has no nontrivial ideals. How about "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "M _ { n } ( \\mathbb { R } ) ?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.644,
            0.871,
            0.812
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Let "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\varkappa"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " be a Banach algebra but do not assume that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\alpha"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has an identity. If I is a left ideal of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\alpha ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " say that I is a modular left ideal if there is a u in "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\varkappa"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " such that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { A } ( 1 - u ) \\equiv \\{ a - a u : a \\in \\mathcal { A } \\} \\subseteq I ;"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " call such an element u of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\varkappa"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " a right modular unit for I. Similarly, define right modular ideals and left modular units. Prove the following. (a) If u is a right modular unit for the left ideal I and "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "u { \\in } I ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "I = \\mathcal { A }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (b) Maximal modular left ideals are maximal left ideals. (c) If I is a proper modular left ideal, then I is contained in a maximal left ideal. (d) If I is a proper modular left ideal and u is a modular right unit for I, then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\| \\boldsymbol { u } - \\boldsymbol { x } \\| \\geqslant 1"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " for all x in I and cl I is a proper modular left ideal. (e) Every maximal modular left ideal of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\alpha"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is closed."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.136,
            0.821,
            0.872,
            0.922
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Using the terminology of Exercise 6, let I be an ideal of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\varkappa"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Show: (a) if u is a right modular unit for I and v is a left modular unit for I, then "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "u - v   \\in   I ,"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (b) If I is closed, "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { A } / I"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has an identity if and only if there is a right modular unit and a left modular unit for I. Call an ideal I such that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { A } / I"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " has an identity a modular ideal. An element u such that "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "u + I"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is an identity for "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathcal { A } / I"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is called a modular identity for I."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
